{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>I said</b> — He says to his soul: you have already acknowledged that HaShem is your master and you are obligated to serve Him, and if it is not good for you, after the manner of \"do not be like slaves.\" Rabbi Solomon the Spaniard said: in his prayers, behold yours not mine; I stand before You and for Your glory, not for the reward of my deed. Rabbi Moses said: my good is not an obligation upon You, for I am not worthy of it, like the holy ones who are in the earth. And in my opinion: he asks mercy from HaShem that He do good to the holy ones who are in the earth, and it was necessary to say \"who are in the earth,\" for the holy ones are the angels, as in \"and all holy ones with You,\" and there is much sorrow for those who do not serve You, but serve others; therefore their sorrows multiply upon the holy ones, and behold my good is not upon You — rather give the good to the holy ones and the mighty of the earth in whom is all my desire, and its meaning: all my will is to be like them. And interpreting \"holy ones\" as the patriarchs does not fit the reason of the psalm."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:23.324087967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}